member.summary:
Picks the op without a model call: create without an entry; style when the request is only about how the entry's UI
looks or where it sits (or the "Change the look" chip); adjust when the entry has properties a value change might cover;
else tweak, with its UI files when the request also mentions their look.

Embedded, browser-based code editor for the scripting runtime — a "simplified VS Code" you start in-process and connect to from a browser to author and run scripts.
Usage: var ide = ScriptIde.Host("C:/Scripts"); // or new IdeOptions { ... } Console.WriteLine(ide.Url); // http://127.0.0.1:xxxxx/ // ... browse, edit, get IntelliSense, run scripts ... ide.Stop();
It serves a Monaco-based UI (file tree, editor, full-text search, Run panel) and answers IntelliSense/diagnostics/run requests using the runtime's own compiler and type resolver, so completions reflect exactly what a script can resolve and execute. The server is pure BCL sockets (no ASP.NET, no HttpListener) and binds to loopback only by default. The IDE can read, write and execute code on the host — only expose it on a trusted network ().

Answer {action: create|rename|delete, kind, name, template, path} with {ok, path} or {error}; with an
 also level | set {id, enabled | field, value} | move {id, to} | default {id} | add {source, item, kind, name}
| update {id} | remove {id} with {ok, error, id, path, level}, level being the setup after the action.

Reads the properties of a mode or mod script: its public instance fields, like a MonoBehaviour
inspector. The label is the field name split on camel case, the default its initializer, and a trailing
// min..max help comment gives the range and help text.

             A game-agnostic wrapper that gives a script-defined class a standard script-behaviour
             lifecycle — Start(), Update(dt), Destroy() — plus arbitrary
             named-event dispatch (). It caches which optional methods the class declares,
             so per-frame  costs nothing when a method is absent and never re-resolves.
             Engine-agnostic and IL2CPP-safe (pure VM dispatch via  — no
             coerced delegates).


Hold one per object/instance (each carries its own field state). The host injects context
             the script reads as a field via , e.g. the bound GameObject.
             var behaviour = ScriptBehaviour.Load(compiled).With("self", gameObject);
             behaviour.Start();
             // each frame:  behaviour.Update(dt);
             // on an event: behaviour.Send("OnGoal");   behaviour.Send("OnEnd", won);

One-stop host for a single script behaviour: reads the source (from a file or a virtual
), compiles it, loads its class via ,
runs the lifecycle, and hot-reloads when the source changes. Change detection is
content-based, so a deleted/empty source simply clears the behaviour — no error spam.
Engine-agnostic and IL2CPP-safe — the log sink is an ordinary host delegate, never a
VM-coerced one.

var host = ScriptBehaviourHost.FromWorkspace(ws, "Startup.cs", options, (level, msg) => Debug.Log(msg));
// each frame:           host.Tick(dt);          // reload-if-changed, then Update(dt)
// on your game events:  host.Send("OnGoal");

A lambda expression. Either expression-bodied (x => x + 1) or
block-bodied (x => { return x + 1; }). Untyped parameters are common;
typed parameters are accepted but the MVP doesn't use the type information.

Hook so a host engine (Unity) can pump an  as a
coroutine and signal completion via the  callback.
When set,  bridges IEnumerator awaitables
into a TaskCompletionSource so scripts can await someCoroutine().
Host registers once at startup, e.g.:

ScriptAsyncBuilder.CoroutinePump = (ie, done) => {
    var go = BugpunchRuntime.HostObject;
    return go.StartCoroutine(RunThenSignal(ie, done));
};
